WEBVTT

NOTE
Synthetic caption track for scripts/fixtures/spaced-repetition-demo.mp4.
It is invented teaching content written for local acceptance testing; it is
not a transcript of any real lecture.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
This synthetic lesson introduces spaced repetition, the practice of reviewing
material at expanding intervals.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
The core claim is simple: memory decays over time, and a well-timed review
resets that decay before you forget the material completely.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
A common starting schedule is one day, then three days, then one week, then
two weeks, and finally one month between reviews.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Retrieval practice is what makes the schedule work: you recall the answer from
memory first, and only then check whether you were right.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Rereading a page feels fluent and comfortable, but that fluency is an illusion
that fades as soon as the page is closed.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
A question you answer correctly moves further into the future; a question you
get wrong returns to the beginning of the schedule.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Interleaving different topics inside one session improves transfer, because
you must first decide which method the problem calls for.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Keep each review card to a single idea, so a wrong answer tells you exactly
which fact needs another pass.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Track the interval length rather than the study minutes: intervals are what
change the strength of the memory.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
To summarise: expand the intervals, retrieve before you review, interleave
topics, and let failures pull a card back to the start.
